      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-ink">{d.scienceTitle}</h2>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-muted mt-1">{d.scienceSub}</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-8 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed text-body">
      
      <!-- Principle 1: Chrastil Solubility Tuning -->
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-amber-950 text-amber-300 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-mono">1</span>
          Chrastil Thermodynamic Solubility &amp; Density Tuning
        </h3>
        <p>
          In supercritical fluid extraction, the equilibrium solubility S of a solute (expressed in g solute / kg CO2) depends primarily on the solvent density ρ and absolute temperature T according to the classic <strong>Chrastil empirical equation</strong>:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-surface-elevated border border-hairline rounded-xl p-3 font-mono text-[11px] text-ink">
          <p>ln(S) = k · ln(ρ_CO2) + (a / T) + b</p>
          <p class="text-muted mt-1">where k = association number (solvation complex), a = ΔH_sol / R, b = empirical constant</p>
        </div>
        <p>
          By operating between 85 and 105 bar at 40°C–50°C, the supercritical CO2 density is precisely maintained at <strong>320 to 420 kg/m³</strong>. Under this low-density window, non-polar monoterpene hydrocarbons (such as D-limonene, α-pinene, and myrcene) dissolve with high solubility (>45 g/kg CO2), whereas high-molecular-weight oxygenated aldehydes, esters, and alcohols exhibit significantly lower partition coefficients. Consequently, monoterpenes are rapidly stripped overhead into the ascending supercritical gas phase, while precious flavor compounds remain concentrated in the descending liquid film.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Principle 2: HETP Mass Transfer in Structured Wire Gauze -->
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-amber-950 text-amber-300 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-mono">2</span>
          High-Efficiency Structured Gauze (HETP &lt; 15 cm)
        </h3>
        <p>
          Unlike random dumping or bubble-cap trays, <strong>Sulzer CY woven stainless steel wire gauze packing</strong> (specific surface area <em>a_p</em> &asymp; 900 m&sup2;/m&sup3;) utilizes capillary suction along microscopic wire intersections to ensure complete, uniform surface wetting across the entire column diameter, even at low liquid spray rates (<em>L</em> &lt; 0.5 m&sup3;/m&sup2;&middot;h):
        </p>
        <div class="bg-surface-elevated border border-hairline rounded-xl p-3 font-mono text-[11px] text-ink">
          <p>HETP = Z_packed / NTU &nbsp;[Height Equivalent to a Theoretical Plate]</p>
          <p class="text-muted mt-1">HETP_CY = 10 to 15 cm &nbsp;|&nbsp; 8.5 m column yields &gt; 55 theoretical separation stages</p>
        </div>
        <p>
          This ultra-high separation stage density ensures that even close-boiling terpenes and sesquiterpenes (such as β-caryophyllene and valencene) can be sharply separated without flooding the column or requiring excessive column heights.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Principle 3: Non-Thermal Deterpenation vs. Vacuum Boiling -->
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-amber-950 text-amber-300 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-mono">3</span>
          Non-Thermal Flavor Stability vs. Thermal Distillation
        </h3>
        <p>
          Conventional commercial citrus oil folding relies on vacuum fractional distillation at pot temperatures exceeding <strong>110°C to 145°C</strong>. Under sustained thermal stress, delicate aliphatic aldehydes (e.g., octanal, decanal, and citral) suffer irreversible degradation:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-xs">
          <li><strong>Citral Cyclization:</strong> Heat and trace moisture catalyze citral (neral/geranial) cyclization into bitter, turpentine-like <em>p-cymene</em> and α,p-dimethylstyrene.</li>
          <li><strong>Aldehyde Oxidation:</strong> High temperatures oxidize fresh citrus aldehydes into rancid octanoic and decanoic fatty acids.</li>
          <li><strong>Pyrrole Off-Notes:</strong> Thermal caramelization of residual plant proteins creates burnt, rubbery sensory defects.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Because SFE-CC operates entirely at non-thermal temperatures (<strong>35°C to 48°C</strong>) under an inert, oxygen-free supercritical CO2 atmosphere, thermal degradation is strictly <strong>0.0%</strong>, preserving the 100% natural, freshly-squeezed orchard aroma profile.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Principle 4: ASME Section VIII & Phototoxic Furocoumarin Removal -->
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-amber-950 text-amber-300 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-mono">4</span>
          Furocoumarin Clean Removal &amp; ASME Pressure Safety
        </h3>
        <p>
          Cold-pressed citrus oils (particularly bergamot, lime, and grapefruit) contain high concentrations of phototoxic furocoumarins—principally <strong>5-methoxypsoralen (5-MOP / bergapten, 1,500–3,500 ppm)</strong>. When exposed to sunlight (UVA 320–400 nm), bergapten intercalates into human epidermal DNA, causing severe blisters, hyperpigmentation, and photomutagenesis:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-surface-elevated border border-hairline rounded-xl p-3 font-mono text-[11px] text-ink">
          <p>IFRA &amp; European Pharmacopoeia 10.0 Standard: Bergapten &lt; 1.0 ppm</p>
          <p class="text-emerald-400 mt-1">SFE-CC Performance: &lt; 0.5 ppm residual bergapten in refined essence</p>
        </div>
        <p>
          In SFE-CC, heavy furocoumarins have negligible solubility in supercritical CO2 at 90 bar and remain trapped in the bottom wax cut, achieving complete non-chemical furocoumarin elimination. For mechanical integrity, the extraction column is forged from certified AISI 316L stainless steel per <strong>ASME Section VIII Div 1 &amp; 2</strong>, engineered with dual spring-loaded relief valves (SRV set at 250 bar) and rupture disc sensors to prevent overpressurization under hydraulic surge.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
